Transition metal sulfides (TMS) are of great interest as promising battery-type electrode materials, however, the poor conductivity and sluggish reaction kinetics seriously limit their application. Here, we designed a hollow structured precursor of Ni-based metal-organic frameworks (Ni-MOFs) via Ostwald ripening mechanism. Based on this unique precursor, a hollow carbon-coated nickel sulfide nanocrystal $\mathrm{(H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C)}$ with sulfur vacancies was further synthesized through an ion exchange strategy and thermal annealing. By optimizing the content of sulfur source, the sample with appropriate S-vacancies $\mathrm{(H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50)}$ was developed. Benefiting from its hollow structure and S-vacancies, this $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ displayed a high reversible specific capacity $(1728\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , 1 $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{~A~g~}}^{-1})$ , stable cycling $(72\%$ capacity retention over 8000 cycles) and superior rate capability. After assembling the asymmetric supercapacitor, a high energy density of 36.88 Wh ${\bf k g}^{-1}$ was achieved. Experimental results and DFT calculations demonstrate that introducing S-vacancies builds an embedded electric field and produces lattice distortions in $\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}S_{\mathrm{1-X}}/\mathrm{C}$ , thus enhancing the conductivity of the material. Our strategy also provides a facile way to construct high-performance TMS with unique hollow structure and S-vacancies for developing advanced energy storage devices.
## 1. Introduction
Supercapacitors have emerged as candidates for the next generation of energy storage devices due to their advantages of high-power density, good cycling stability, and fast charge and discharge time [1–5]. How­ ever, the low energy density limits their practical application compared to batteries [6–10]. Therefore, seeking the high-performance electrode materials is still highly desired. Owning to the high redox voltage and high capacity, transition metal sulfides (TMS) have been widely studied as electrode materials for supercapacitors [11–13]. Among the TMS positive materials, NiS has attracted lots of attention due to its high theoretical capacity, non-toxic, and easy fabrication [14–16]. Never­ theless, NiS still suffers from the limited rate performance caused by its poor electrical conductivity and unsatisfactory cycling performance owing to its severe volume variation upon repeated cycling [17,18]. Therefore, it will be promising to enhance the performance of NiS by enhancing its conductivity and suppressing its volume expansion.
Introducing carbon matrix is most commonly-used strategy to address the shortcomings of poor rate performance and cycle perfor­ mance of electrode materials [19–21]. However, directly introducing the external carbon source likely induces the poor interface bonding between the active materials and carbon source. The active materials are easily separated from the carbon substrate, resulting in the unsatisfac­ tory performance. Therefore, forming a stable and strong interface bond between the active materials and the carbon substrate is a key factor to further improve their performance [22]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new type of porous material composed of metal nodes and organic linkers, which have attracted wide attention due to their tune­ able structure, variable composition and regulable porosity [23]. In addition, MOFs have abundant carbon sources and metal ions, they can be used as self-sacrificial templates to synthesize active materials with special designed structures, such as C [24], metal oxide/carbon (M-O/C) [25], metal sulfide/carbon (M-S/C) [26], metal phosphide/carbon (MP/C) [27], etc. In MOF derivatives, metal ions can contact carbon substrate tightly as they come from the same molecule, which effectively overcomes the shortcomings of an external carbon source.
Hollow structures with controllable interior voids and functional exterior shells, which can alleviate the volume expansion of the elec­ trode material during cycling, have earned extensive attention in many fields, such as electrocatalysis [28], lithium-ion batteries [29], and supercapacitors [30]. To date, various strategies have been proposed to synthesize hollow structures, such as soft template, hard template, bubble method, etc. For example, Qu et al. prepared hollow carbon microspheres via a modified template method for sodium-ion batteries. These carbon microspheres possess a high capacity $(334\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at 50 mA ${\bf g}^{-1}.$ ) and stable cycle ability [31]. Liu et al. synthesized hierarchical NiS/N-doped carbon hollow sphere using $\mathrm{{SiO}_{2}}$ as a hard template [32]. The synthetic hollow structure can relieve the volume expansion of the electrode material during charging/discharging. However, this hard template principle is complicated and costly for removing the template. Moreover, the hollow structure is easily to be damaged during the bat­ tery operation. Alternatively, the hollow sphere also can be fabricated by Ostwald ripening process [33]. This thermodynamically-driven spontaneous process occurs because larger particles are more energeti­ cally favored than smaller particles. The hollow spherical structure prepared by Ostwald ripening principle not only overcomes the complicated process of removing the template, but also avoids the destruction of the material structure.
More recently, introducing O or S vacancies to electrode materials has reported to enhance their conductivity and electrochemical perfor­ mance [34]. Furthermore, a built-in electric field would also be induced due to the unbalanced redistribution of charges, which accelerates the migration of ions/electrons [35]. Liu et al. produced abundant S-va­ cancies in $\mathrm{CoNi_{2}S_{4}}$ by the $\mathrm{{NaBH_{4}}}$ reduction, the reduced $\mathrm{CoNi_{2}S_{4}}$ nanosheet delivers a high capacity of $1918\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ [36]. Wang et al. developed S-vacancies in $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}S_{4}}$ through the plasma induction, $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}}{\cal S}_{4}$ with 30 s plasma processing exhibits much higher capacities $(782.3\mathrm{C}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.5\mathrm{A}\ g^{-1})$ than that without S-vacancies [37]. Inspired by previous reports, we believe that introducing vacancies into TMS materials would benefit to the enhancement of their performance in supercapacitor applications. However, long experimental period and harsh reaction conditions by the hydrothermal reduction and plasma induction methods are still challenging. Therefore, it is still urgent to develop new strategies to introduce controllable vacancies in mentalbased electrode material.
Herein, we fabricated a hollow structured NiS $\left(\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{1-X}}{/}\mathrm{C}\right)$ with uniform carbon coating and abundant S-vacancies by hiring the hollow Ni-based MOF as the precursor. A proper amount of S-vacancies was introduced into NiS through thermal annealing, which was convinced by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and evolutionary power re­ actors (EPR). Resulting from the unique structure and compositional advantages, $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ display a high reversible specific capacity $(1728\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $1\mathrm{A}\mathfrak{g}^{-1}\rbrack$ , stable cycling $(72\%$ capacity retention over 8000 cycles) and superior rate capability. Density functional theory (DFT) calculations confirm that NiS with S-vacancies features a superior con­ ductivity compared to NiO and NiS without vacancies. Our strategy provides insightful view to design other hollow spherical TMS with Svacancies for high-performance energy storage systems, such as lithium/ sodium-ion batteries and aqueous zinc batteries.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Nickel Nitrate hexahydrate (AR, $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ $\mathrm{6H_{2}O}\mathrm{\dot{_{2}}}$ ), Trimestic acid (AR, $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC})$ , and Thioaceamide $(\mathsf{A R},\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{5}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{S})$ were purchased from Tianjin Damao chemical Reagent Co. All chemicals in the company are used without further processing, the deionized water is used during the experiment. The activated carbon (Surface area: $1800\ (\pm100)\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and pore volume: $1.0{-}1.2\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ used in this work was purchased from Nanjing Xianfeng Nano Material Technology Co., Ltd.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Ni-MOF
$430\ \mathrm{mg\Ni(NO_{3})_{2}\ 6H}$ $6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $150~\mathrm{mg}$ trimesic acid, $1.0\mathrm{~g~PVP}$ were dis­ solved in a mixed solution of $40~\mathrm{mL}$ absolute ethanol and deionized water $\mathrm{{(V:V=1:1}^{\cdot}}$ ). Stirring for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ to mix them. The solution was transferred to an autoclave at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ . It was collected by centrifugation, washed three times with deionized water and ethanol, and then dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain green Ni-MOF.
