## 2.6. Electrochemical measurements
The electrochemical performance was tested by a CHI660D electro­ chemical workstation (Shanghai, Chenhua). In the three-electrode sys­ tem, the slurry is dispersed in NMP using $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{1{-}\mathrm{X}}/\mathsf{C}{-}50$ , PVDF, and conductive carbon black at the mass ratio of is 8:1:1. The slurry was coated on the nickel foam, and then dried in a vacuum drying oven at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain the working electrode. The mass load is $\sim1.5\:\mathrm{mg}\:\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Meanwhile, a Pt foil, ${\mathrm{Hg/HgO}}$ and 6 M KOH was treated as the counter electrode, reference electrode and the electrolyte, respectively. In addition, an asymmetric supercapacitor was assembled using $\mathrm{H}{\mathrm{-Ni}}\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{1-X}}/$ C-50 as the positive electrode, AC as the negative electrode, and KOH/ PVA as the gel electrolyte. The KOH/PVA gel electrolyte was prepared according to traditional methods. Firstly, $_{3\mathrm{~g~}}$ PVA was dispersed in DI $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{30~mL}$ ) and mixed for $60\mathrm{min}$ until PVA was completely dissolved to generate a crystal gelatinous liquid. Afterward, $_{3\textrm{g}}$ KOH was added gradually to the gelatinous liquid and mixed for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ to obtain the KOH/PVA gel electrolyte. Then, the gel electrolyte was applied to nickel foams loaded active substances. Finally, two nickel foams were assem­ bled the asymmetric device in face-to-face with special separator and
then pressed under $0.2\mathrm{MPa}$
According to the charge balance theory, the specific capacitance is $180\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ for AC and $1728\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ at the potential windows $-1.0{-}0\mathrm{~V~}$ for AC and $_{0-0.5\mathrm{~V~}}$ for $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ as well as at current density $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . The mass ration of $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{1{-}\mathrm{X}{/}}\mathsf{C}{-}50$ to the AC was calculated to be 1:4.8, thus the load capacity of a traditional asymmetric supercapacitor is about $8.5~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{(H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ : $\sim1.5~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ AC: ${\sim}7~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ .
According to following formulas, the specific capacitance in the three system was analyzed from GCD curves.
$$
s c=\frac{I\times\Delta t}{m\times\Delta V}
$$
where Δt, ΔV, m and $I$ are representative the discharging time (s), the potential window (V), and the active material mass $\mathbf{\sigma}({\bf g})$ , current density (A), respectively.
According to the charge balance theory $({\mathsf{q}}+={\mathsf{q}}-)$ , the mass ratio of the positive electrode material and the negative electrode material was optimized, thereby increasing the energy density of the supercapacitor.
$$
\begin{array}{c}{q=s c\times m\times\Delta V}\\ {\displaystyle\frac{m_{+}}{m_{-}}=\frac{s c_{-}}{s c_{+}}\times\frac{\Delta V_{-}}{\Delta V_{+}}}\end{array}
$$
where $m,s c$ , and $\Delta V$ represent the active material mass (g), the specific capacitance $(\mathrm{F}~\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ , and the potential window (V), respectively.
The energy density and power density of asymmetric supercapacitors are obtained by the following formula.
$$
\begin{array}{c}{{s c=\displaystyle\frac{I\times\Delta t}{m\times\Delta V}}}\\ {{}}\\ {{E=\displaystyle\frac{s c\times\Delta V^{2}}{2\times3.6}}}\\ {{}}\\ {{P=\displaystyle\frac{E}{t}\times3600}}\end{array}
$$
where $m,s c,\Delta V,E$ and $P$ represent the active material mass (g), the specific capacitance $(\mathrm{F~g}^{-1})$ , the potential window (V), energy density (Wh $\mathbf{\mathrm{Kg}}^{-1})$ and power density $(\mathrm{W~Kg}^{-1})$ respectively.
## 2.7. Density functional theory calculations method
DFT calculations were conducted to optimize crystalline structures and calculate the potential energies total electronic densities of states for
S-Ni and O-Ni. The functional of Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) was employed as the exchange–correlation potentials. The projected augmented wave (PAW) pseudopotentials were employed to represent the electron–ion interactions. A kinetic energy cutoff of $520\mathrm{eV}$ was set to expand the monoelectronic states in plane waves. The self-consistent field (SCF) convergence criterion was set to $1.0\times10^{-5}\mathrm{eV}$ . For structure optimizations, the ionic relaxation criterion was set to $0.01~\mathrm{eV}/\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ . $14\times$ $14\times14$ , $11\times11\times6$ Monkhorst-Pack k-point mesh was adopted for ONi and S-Ni. DFT calculations were performed using the Vienna ab initio simulation package (VASP) 5.4.4.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphology and structure characterization
Ni-based hollow materials were prepared by solvothermal method using nickel nitrate as the nickel source, trimesic acid as the organic ligand, and Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) as the stabilizer. The synthesis procedure was schematically illustrated in Fig. 1. Firstly, the nickelbased MOF (Ni-MOF) with hollow structure was decorated with coneshaped structures through a facile solvothermal process [33]. This hol­ low Ni-MOF was treated as under different processes. In the stage I, the Ni-MOF precursor undergoes a heat treatment process under ${\bf N}_{2}$ the metal nickel salt is reduced to nickel metal, and the organic ligands are carbonized to form Ni/C. After the oxidation treatment, an NiO/C maintained the hollow spherical structure was obtained. In the stage II, the precursor of Ni-MOF would be treated with $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{5}N S}$ , and generate NiS@MOF through the ion exchange. Subsequently, the carbon-coated NiS with hollow structure and S-vacancies $\left(\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{1-X}}/\mathsf{C}\right)$ was obtained via carbonization treatment. The reaction equations can be expressed as follows [38].
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{C H_{3}C S N H_{2}+C_{2}H_{5}O H=C H_{3}(N H_{2})C(C O C_{2}H_{5})-S H}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{C H_{3}(N H_{2})C(C O C_{2}H_{5})-S H+C_{2}H_{5}O H=C H_{3}(N H_{2})C(C O C_{2}H_{5})_{2}+H_{2}S}}\end{array}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{H_{2}S=2H^{+}+S^{2-}}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{N i^{2+}+S^{2-}=N i S}}\end{array}
$$
The influence of the hydrothermal time on the structure of precursor was studied. In order to understand the formation mechanism of the hollow structure during the solvothermal process of Ni-MOF, we studied its solvothermal reaction kinetics process. After $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ of reaction, solid NiMOF sphere with smooth surface is prepared, as shown in the
Transmission Electron Microscope (TEM) image (Fig. 2a). As the reac­ tion time extends to $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the sphere becomes hollow and the surface roughness increases (Fig. 2b). When the reaction time increases to $10\mathrm{{h}}$ , a completely hollow structure is obtained (Fig. 2c), indicating sol­ vothermal time has a great influence on the formation of hollow struc­ ture. X-ray diffraction (XRD) results (Fig. 2d–f) demonstrate that the crystallinity of products becomes strong along with the increase of sol­ vothermal reaction time. Under solvothermal reaction conditions, the inside of the sphere gradually dissolves and diffuses to the surface, eventually forming a hollow sphere structure during the crystallization process, as illustrated in Fig. 2g.
To study the S resource effects on NiS performance, different con­ tents of thioacetamide were added during the stage II. According to the content of thioacetamide $\mathrm{30~mg}$ , $50~\mathrm{mg}$ , and $80~\mathrm{mg})$ , the obtained products are marked as $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}30}$ , $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{1}{-}\mathrm{X}}/\mathsf{C}{-}50$ , and $\mathrm{H}{\mathrm{-NiS}_{\mathrm{1-X}}}/\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}$ 80, respectively. XRD measurements were first conducted to evaluate the crystal structure of various samples, as shown in Fig. 3a. Three H$\mathrm{NiS_{1-X}/C}$ samples show similar peaks at $30.4^{\circ}$ , $34.9^{\circ}$ , $46.0^{\circ}$ and $53.7^{\circ}$ , which is assigned to crystal planes (1 0 0), (1 0 1), (1 0 2), and (1 1 0) of NiS (JCPDS No. 65-3419), respectively. It can be seen that each product features the high peak intensity, indicating their good crystallinity. The result shows that the content of thioacetamide has no obvious effect on the crystal structure of the material. However, the highest diffraction angle shift can be observed at the peak, indicating that the lattice shrinkage caused by S-vacancies [39].
In order to calculate the carbon content in these products, ther­ mogravimetric analysis (TGA) was performed in the air (Fig. S1, Sup­ porting Information). The thermogravimetric analysis showed a weight loss when the temperature is lower than $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{i}}$ , which can be attributed to the evaporation of water absorbed in the material. Under the air environment, the obvious weight loss of $300\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}500\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ in the ther­ mogravimetric curve is attributed to the oxidation of carbon to form carbon dioxide. The carbon content in the $\mathrm{H}{\mathrm{-NiS_{1-X}/C}}$ is about $18\%$ . The obvious weight loss at $600{-}800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is due to the formation of NiO during the conversion reaction [40]. Raman spectra were collected in Fig. 3b. The peak centered at $1350\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1590~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is commonly denoted as D and G band, which arises from the disordered structure and bondstretching motion of $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ hybridized carbon, respectively [41]. Raman spectrum of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}30}$ , $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{1}{-}\mathrm{X}}/\mathsf{C}{-}50$ , $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}80}$ , and NiO/C shows an $\mathrm{I_{G}/I_{D}}$ peak intensity ration is 1.25, 1.23, 1.18, and 1.01, respectively. Peak intensity ratio indicates that the annealing process helps to generate the graphitic carbon.
To investigate the surface area and pore size distribution of the asprepared samples, the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) analysis has been performed (Fig. 3c and Fig. S2). $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ displays a meso­ porous structure with typical IV nitrogen adsorption/desorption iso­ therms with a BET surface area of $27.3\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}^{2}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ . The specific surface area of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}30}$ , $\mathrm{H-NiS_{1-X}/C-80}$ , and NiO/C is $28\bar{.3}\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $24.8~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}^{-1}$ , and $70.1\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The pore size of all samples is mostly distributed between 5 and $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which not only facilitates electron transfer, but also allows the electrolyte to easily enter and transport during the charge and discharge process, thereby helping to improve the electrochemical performance.
XPS measurements were carried on various samples, as shown in Fig. 3d–f and Fig. S3a–d. The $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ composite contains Ni 2p, O 1s, C 1s, $\mathtt{s2p}$ in the full spectrum. For $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{1{-}\mathrm{X}{\mathord{\left/{\vphantom{-}\mathrm{X}{-}\mathrm{X}{-}\mathrm{G}{-}\mathrm{S}{0}}}\right.\kern-\nulldelimiterspace}\mathrm{X}{-}\mathrm{X}{\mathord{\left/{\vphantom{-}\mathrm{S}{0}}\right.\kern-\nulldelimiterspace}\mathrm{C}{-}\mathrm{S}{0}}}$ , the Ni 2p spectrum (Fig. 3d) exhibits different oxidation states related to $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ , two doublets positioned at 853.2 /870.52 eV and at $855.1/873.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ agree with $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ of H-NiS1-X/C-50 [39]. For the S 2p curve in Fig. 3e, three main peaks at $161.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ , $162.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ , and $163.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ are observed, which can be attributed to $\begin{array}{r}{s2\mathtt{p}_{3/2},}\end{array}$ $\}2\mathrm{p}_{1/2},$ and C-S, respectively. The peak at $161.8\mathrm{eV}$ is representative of mental-sulfur bonds, while the peak at $162.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ should be ascribed to sulfur ions at low coordination ordi­ narily related to S-vacancies [39,42,43]. The result confirms the exis­ tence of S-vacancies in NiS hollow spherical structure. The C 1s corelevel XPS spectrum (Fig. 3f) can be deconvoluted into three compo­ nents at 284.8 eV, $286.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ , and $288.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which can be attributed to C–C, C–S, and $\mathtt{C=}0$ [44] groups, respectively, indicating S is suc­ cessfully doped in the carbon material. More importantly, the content of S $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ (relative to $\textsf{s}2\mathrm{p})$ in $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}30}$ is $19.6\%$ , which is much higher than that of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ and $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}80}$ , indicating the highly-concentrated S-vacancies in $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}30}$ (Table S1) [39,45]. As expected, after deconvolution of XPS spectra, the ratio of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}/\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ in $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}30}$ was larger than $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ and $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}80}$ . The low-valence metals further reveals the high content of S-vacancies in H$\mathrm{NiS_{1-X}}/\mathrm{C}{\cdot}30$ (Table S1) [46]. However, introducing excessive vacancies will cause the structural destruction of materials, which affects their electrochemical performance [47]. The vacancy-rich samples, with profound crystal disordering, likely transform into an amorphous phase during the charge–discharge process. This amorphous phase is of a larger resistance compared with that of well crystalline structures. Therefore, samples that are of high vacancies concentration cannot maintain the capacitance after long-term cycles due to the increased resistance [45]. The EPR measurement, an intuitive and advanced technology for detecting and characterizing O/S vacancies in nano­ materials, was performed to further explore S-vacancies (Fig. 3g). The H$\mathrm{NiS_{1-X}/C-X}$ $[\mathrm{X}=30$ , 50, 80) composite showed the sharp and strong EPR signal at $g=2.0$ , showing the presence of S-vacancies [48]. From the response signal of the EPR curves, the response signal is obviously enhanced with the decrease of the amount of thioacetamide, indicating that the content of S-vacancies is the highest in $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}30}$ , which is consistent with XPS results. The joint analysis of XPS and EPR shows that there is an interaction between S-vacancies and electrons in the H-NiS1- $x/\mathsf{C}\cdot50$ complex, which promotes the interface charge transfer and re­ action kinetics. The formation mechanism of S-vacancies in NiS could be expressed in Fig. 3h. During the thermal annealing process, S atoms in NiS can easily undergo an oxidation–reduction reaction with C atoms to form the S-vacancies. By introducing of self-adaptive S-vacancies in NiS, an unbalance of the charge distribution on the interface between NiS and C can be built, which possibly affects the electrochemical perfor­ mance of NiS.
In addition, the contact angle (CA) of the prepared materials was tested (Fig. S4). The CA of NiO/C, $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}30}$ , $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{1{-}\mathrm{X}}/\mathsf{C}{-}50$ , H$\mathrm{NiS_{1-X}/C-80}$ with 6 M KOH electrolyte was $82^{\circ}$ , $74^{\circ}$ , $70^{\circ}$ , and $66^{\circ}$ , respectively. With the increase of thioacetamide, the CA decreased gradually, indicating that the electrode material became more hydro­ philic, which may be attributed to the doping of sulfur atoms. Good wettability allows full contact between electrolyte and electrode mate­ rials, resulting in the enhanced electrochemical charge storage [49]. The morphology of as-prepared samples was detected by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and TEM. The synthesized Ni-MOF precursor shows a uniform spherical structure with a relatively smooth surface (Fig. 4a). The high-resolution SEM image (Fig. 4b) shows that its surface is modified by some needle-like substances [33]. The size of the synthetic ball is about $2\mathrm{-}3~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . It can be seen from the broken ball that the syn­ thetic ball has a hollow spherical structure (Fig. 4a inset). The Ni-MOF hierarchical hollow structure was further confirmed by TEM image (Fig. 4c). SEM images of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ were shown in Fig. 4d-e. H-NiS1- $\mathrm{_x/C-50}$ maintains the hollow spherical structure of the precursor with uniform size of $1~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Compared with the precursor, the sphere size is significantly reduced (Fig. 4f) and the surface is composed of small particles, which may be related to the decomposition of the organic ligand after carbonization. SEM images of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}30}$ , $\mathrm{H-NiS_{1-X}/C-}$ 80, and NiO/C were shown in Fig. S5a-d. These materials present uni­ form hollow spherical structure, indicating that the material has good structural stability. HRTEM image (Fig. 4g) reveals that the NiS nano­ crystal shows clear lattice fringes with d-spacing of $0.19~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and 0.26 nm, corresponding to the (101) and (102) planes of NiS, respectively [50]. As expected, HRTEM image reveals that inner parts of the sphere show that nanoparticles with crystal lattices are dispersed on the carbon matrix. This can ease NiS particles from growing larger and enhance the stability of the structure. The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern shown in Fig. 4h confirms the existence of the poly-crystalline NiS. TEM-EDS elemental mapping images (Fig. 4i-j) show that the C, Ni and S elements are homogeneously distributed on $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ , which is consistent with XPS results.
## 3.2. Electrochemical properties
The electrochemical behaviors of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}30}$ , $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}_{;}$ H$\mathrm{NiS_{1-X}/C-80}$ NiO/C in 6 M KOH electrolyte was evaluated in a threeelectrode system by cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic charge/ discharge (GCD), electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). The CV curves were collected under a scan rate of $5\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ within a voltage window $_{0-0.5\mathrm{~V~}}$ (Fig. 5a), all materials show a pair of obvious redox peaks, demonstrating that their good pseudocapacitance behavior. Evidently, the integrated area under CV curve of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ is much larger that of others, suggesting that $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{1{-}\mathrm{X}{/}}\mathsf{C}{-}50$ has a superior capacitance. At the same time, GCD was performed to measure the ca­ pacity of these electrodes at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ (Fig. 5b). All curves have good symmetry, indicating their high reversibility. Two distinct voltage pla­ teaus in good agreement with the redox peaks are observed. CV curves of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ under various scan rates of $5{-}50\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ were shown in Fig. 5c. It appears as a pair of obvious redox peaks. The related redox reaction can be attributed to the following equations [32].
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{N i S+O H^{-}=N i S O H+e^{-}}}\\ {{}}\\ {{N i S O H+O H^{-}=N i S O+H_{2}O+e^{-}\ (O x i d a t i o n r e a c t i o n)}}\\ {{}}\\ {{N i S O+H_{2}O+e^{-}=N i S O H+O H^{-}}}\\ {{}}\\ {{N i S O H+e^{-}=N i S+O H^{-}\ (\mathrm{Re}d u c t i o n r e a c t i o n)}}\end{array}
$$
The redox peaks are obvious especially at $5\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . With the scan rate increases, the peak shape remains almost unchanged, indicating that this electrode features good reversibility. CV curves of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{H-NiS}}_{1\cdot{\mathrm{X}}}/{\mathrm{C}}-$ 3 $\mathrm{\Delta),H-NiS_{1-X}/C-80}$ , and NiO/C under different $\mathsf{5}{-}50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ are shown in Fig. S6a–c. The redox peak and the related redox reaction are the same as that of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ , however, the peak shape of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}30}$ and $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}80}$ was seriously deformed with the increase of scan rate, which suggests the poor rate performance. GCD curves of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ at different current densities were shown in Fig. 5d. According to Eq.(4), the specific capacitance was calculated to be $1728\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ , $1400\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ , $1290\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $11\bar{5}0\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $1100\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $2\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , ${\mathsf{\Pi}}_{5\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}}$ , 8 A ${\bf g}^{-1}$ and $10\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ , respectively. These values are higher than these of H$\mathrm{NiS_{1-X}}/\mathrm{C}{\cdot}30$ , $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}80}$ , NiO/C electrode (Fig. S6d–f), and even higher than those of NiS-based materials under the same conditions (summarized in Fig. S7). The dynamic behaviors of the ion/electron migration of all electrode materials in our work were characterized by EIS. All the Nyquist plots (Fig. 5e) are comprised with a small-semicircle in high frequency section and an incline at low frequency region, rep­ resenting the interfacial redox activities and diffusion-related process, respectively. Notably, Nyquist plots of the $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ possesses smaller semicircle and steeper line than these of other samples, alluding the faster charge transfer and higher ion diffusion rate, respectively. This faster electrochemical response would contribute to better rate abilities. The rate performance of all materials was compared in Fig. 5f. As the current density increases, the specific capacitance slightly decreases. The cycling stability of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ was evaluated by repeating the CV tests at $50\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ (Fig. 5g). After 8000 cycles, the retention of specific capacitance is $72\%$ . Based on the above results, we believe that the excellent electrochemical performance of $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{1{-}\mathrm{X}{/}}\mathsf{C}{-}50$ electrode is consistent with the well-designed nanostructure. As illustrated by Fig. S8 it schematically reflects the $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions/electrons migration pro­ cess of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ , in which $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions can shuttle easily through the gaps manifests the fast frequency response. Therefore, the excellent electrochemical performance of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ can be attributed to the following points: Firstly, the hollow structure alleviates the volume expansion during cycling, which effectively maintains the stability of the electrode structure. Benefiting from this hollow structure, ions only need to diffuse in the shell. Thus, the diffusion process from the outer shell to the inner shell could be avoided, which contributes to the enhancement of reaction kinetics. In addition, the carbon-coated TMS materials derived from the MOF realize tightly integration on a molecular level and effectively avoid the separation of composite materials during repeated cycling. Moreover, S-vacancies not only effectively improve the conductivity of electrode materials, but also increase the content of low-oxidation state cations, resulting in more electrochemically active sites.
In order to deep understand the electrochemical reaction kinetics of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ electrode, we further studied the effects of two different charge storage processes based on the CV curves (Fig. 5h). It is well known that the total charge storage of an electrode consists of two parts: diffusion-controlled reaction and surface-controlled capacitance. Ac­ cording to following formulas, the faradic contribution and charge storage were estimated at different scan rates.
$$
i=a\nu^{b}
$$
$$
l o g i=l o g a+b l o g\nu
$$
here, $i$ and $\nu$ corresponds to the peak current and scan rate, respectively. If ${\bf b}\le0.5$ , the capacitance contribution corresponds to the response of diffusion control. If ${\bf b}=1$ , it will be the response of surface control. The electrochemical process of diffusion or surface control can be easily analyzed according to the value of b. As shown in Fig. 5h, the b value of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}30}$ , $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ , $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}80}$ , and NiO/C is 0.66, 0.74, 0.65, and 0.68, respectively. This shows that the electrochemical reac­ tion of the $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{1{-}\mathrm{X}}/\mathsf{C}{-}50$ composite in the KOH electrolyte is related to both the diffusion-controlled (battery-type) process and the surfacecontrolled (capacitive-type) process [8].
The electronic structures of NiO and NiS were studied using DFT simulations. The structural model of NiO and NiS was constructed based on the results of XRD, as shown in Fig. 5i–l and Table S3. The calculated lattice parameters are similar to the experimental values. Its band structure can be explained as O 2p orbital and Ni 3d orbits, which was used as the valence band (VB) and conduction band (CB), respectively. From the analysis of TDOS and PDOS of NiS and NiO (Fig. 5m and n), the energy band of NiS near the Fermi level is mainly dominated by the Ni 3d state. In NiO, however, O 2p state is the majority. In addition, the energy band gap of NiS is smaller that of NiO. This shows that NiS has better conductivity than NiO, which is consistent with the EIS results. Good conductivity benefits to the transmission of electrons, which is the main reason why the electrochemical performance of NiS is superior to NiO. In addition, Fig. 5o shows the DOS with S-vacancies and perfect crystals. It can be seen that the top of the valence band maximum (VBM) shifts up when S-vacancies exist, indicating that NiS with S-vacancies has higher conductivity than that without S-vacancies. Theoretical calculations and experimental results jointly proved that introducing a proper amount of S-vacancies in the NiS can improve the conductivity of the material, thereby improving its electrochemical performance.
In order to evaluate the practical application of $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ , an asymmetric supercapacitor is assembled by coupling with the active carbon (AC) (Fig. 6a). CV and GCD curves of AC electrode were dis­ played in Fig. S9a–b, showing its specific capacitance of $180\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1 A ${\mathbf g}^{-1}$ . As shown in Fig. 6b, the voltage window of AC and $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ is $-1.0$ to $0\mathrm{v}$ and 0 to $0.5\mathrm{V}$ , respectively. And the voltage window of the assembled asymmetric supercapacitor is $-1.0$ to $0.5\mathrm{V}_{\cdot}$ . CV plots of the H$\mathrm{NiS}_{\mathrm{1-X}}/\mathrm{C}{\cdot}50//\mathrm{AC}$ (ASC) under different voltage windows at $50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ were shown in Fig. 6c. As the voltage window gradually extended to 1.6 V, the polarization of the ASC can be found. Therefore, the stable voltage window of the ASC can be expanded to $1.5\mathrm{V}$ without outgoing polari­ zation. From Fig. 6d, the CV shape of solid-state ASC device is preserved well even at $100\mathrm{mVs}^{-1}$ , indicating its nature of fast charge/discharge. All GCD curves at various current densities (Fig. 6e) display the quasisymmetric feature, revealing excellent electrochemical reversibility and good coulombic efficiency. Moreover, the specific capacitance is $118\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $101\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $90\mathrm{F}\ g^{-1}$ , $88\mathrm{F}{\bf g}^{-1}$ , $85\mathrm{F}\ g^{-1}$ , $82\mathrm{F}\ g^{-1}$ , and $78\mathrm{F}\ g^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $2\mathrm{A}\ g^{-1}$ , $5\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $8\mathrm{A}\ g^{-1}$ , $10\mathrm{A}\ g^{-1}$ , $12{\mathrm{A}}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ , and $15\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively. Fig. 6f shows specific capacitances of ASC at different current densities. As the current density increases, the specific capaci­ tance decreases slightly, indicating its outstanding rate performance. The cycling stability of this device was measured by repeating the CV tests at $50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ (Fig. 6g). After 5000 cycles, the retention is $75\%$ , and the CV shapes have no obvious difference between the 1st and 5000th cycle (Fig. 6g inset). Ragone plots of ASC $\mathrm{H-NiS_{1-X}}/\mathrm{C}{\cdot}50//\mathrm{AC}$ was pre­ sented in Fig. 6h. Apparently, a high energy density of 36.88 Wh $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ was achieved when the power density of ASC $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{1{-}\mathrm{X}}/\mathsf{C}{-}50//\mathsf{A}$ C was 750 W $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Kg}}^{-1}$ . Even under the power density of 7465 W $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Kg}}^{-1}$ , it still maintains a high energy density of 22.81 Wh $\mathbf{\mathrm{Kg}}^{-1}$ . These values are also superior to other previous reported devices, such as NiS microflowers// AC (31 Wh $\mathbf{\mathrm{Kg}}^{-1}$ at 900 W $\mathbf{K}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{\bar{\Sigma}}$ ) [14], NiS//AC (16.5 Wh $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ at 250 W $\mathbf{\mathrm{Kg}}^{-1})$ [51], NiS Hollow Cub//CNF (34.9 Wh $\mathbf{\mathrm{Kg}}^{-1}$ at 387.5 W $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}.$ ) [38], $\mathrm{NiCo}_{2}S_{4}@\mathrm{GA}//\mathrm{AC}$ (20.9 Wh $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ at 800.2 W $\mathbf{\mathrm{Kg}}^{-1}$ ) [52], $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}}S_{4}/\mathrm{CF}//\mathrm{CNF}$ (28.8 Wh $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ at 878.3 W $\mathbf{K}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ [53], To evaluate the practicability of ASC $\mathrm{H-NiS_{1-X}/C-}50//\mathrm{AC}$ , a blue light-emitting diode (LED) with ${\sim}1.5\mathrm{v}$ working voltage can be lightened by two solid-state devices (Fig. 6h inset).
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we designed a hollow spherical Ni-MOFs precursor through Ostwald ripening mechanism. Based on this unique precursor, a hollow $\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}S_{\mathrm{1-X}}/\mathrm{C}$ with S-vacancies and uniform carbon coating was further synthesized through an ion exchange strategy and thermal annealing. By regulating the amount sulfur resource during hydrother­ mal reaction, the amount of S-vacancies in $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C}$ could be controlled, which has been confirmed by XPS and EPR measurements. Svacancies and S doping-C produces a built-in electric field in $\mathrm{H-NiS_{1-X}/C-}$ 50, which accelerates the migration of ions/electrons. In addition, Svacancies also help to improve the conductivity of NiS, as further confirmed by DFT calculations. Based on the structural integrity and compositional advantages, $\mathrm{H{-}N i S_{1-X}/C{-}50}$ displayed a high performance in both three-electrode system and asymmetric device. The work pre­ sents a successful method to regulate the electronic structure of MTS to achieve excellent performance and reveals a great application prospect to improve energy storage device based on metal sulfides composites.
